Title:
LetMeowIn

Type:
GitHub Tool

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
- LetMeowIn is a Windows x64 LSASS credential dumper written in C++ with MASM x64, focused on stealthy acquisition of LSASS memory for offline dumping.  
- It aims to reduce EDR/AV detection by avoiding common user-mode API patterns and telemetry, using indirect syscalls/NTAPI usage, API/name obfuscation, and polymorphic compile-time hashing.  
- A key technique is duplicating *existing* LSASS handles rather than opening new ones, then creating an offline copy of the LSASS process to dump from.  
- It also tampers with dump artifacts by corrupting the `MDMP` signature of dropped files to evade simplistic file-based detection.  
- The repo notes historical bypass success against several mainstream products, but also highlights limitations (x64-only and dependency on pre-existing LSASS handles).  
- Useful primarily for red teams/pentesters and malware researchers studying modern LSASS dumping tradecraft and defensive gaps.

Technical Focus:
- LSASS handle duplication (reusing existing handles)
- Indirect system calls / manual NTAPI invocation
- ETW/telemetry disruption
- Compile-time hashing & polymorphic obfuscation
- Offline LSASS process cloning and dumping
- Minidump (`MDMP`) signature corruption / artifact evasion

Use Cases:
- Stealth credential access via LSASS memory dumping on Windows endpoints
- Researching/replicating modern EDR-evasion patterns around process access and dumping
- Developing detections for suspicious handle duplication, LSASS cloning, and dump artifact manipulation
- Adversary emulation of credential dumping tradecraft in controlled assessments

Keywords:
LSASS, credential dumping, Windows x64, C++, MASM, NTAPI, indirect syscalls, syscalls, ETW, telemetry bypass, API obfuscation, compile-time hashing, handle duplication, process cloning, minidump, MDMP signature, EDR evasion, Windows Defender, CrowdStrike Falcon, Cortex XDR